{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gvke25gAurHXkntgthctQs","type":"tool_result","content":"WI-555-wi508-partial-close.md\n---OI-71---\n3029:[open_item.OI-71]\n3030-title = \"The held wi508 lane: merge on the record, one more round, re-scope, or abandon\"\n3031-status = \"ruled\"\n3032-ruled_date = \"2026-08-31\"\n3033-ruling_ref = \"docs/log.d/2026-08-31-owner-rulings-oi70-71-72.md\"\n3034-raised = \"2026-08-31\"\n3035-one_line = \"\"\"RULED ((c), the manual partial close sanctioned) 2026-08-31 — the owner: option (c), the kit's own partial close — effectively equivalent to (d) except nothing is discarded; the state is recorded as partial. Original brief: rule what happens to the 43-commit wi508 lane held since 2026-08-30 (its record is on origin since 2026-08-31, no longer single-copy)\"\"\"\n3036-decision = \"\"\"RULED 2026-08-31 (record docs/log.d/2026-08-31-owner-rulings-oi70-71-72.md): (c). Close the lane through the kit's own partial path; effectively equivalent to (d) except nothing is discarded — the state is recorded as partial and the evidence stays in history. What makes (c) strictly non-lossy now: the branch was pushed to origin on 2026-08-31 (correcting the single-copy claim below), and close_partial commits the lane's work as-is before archiving the spec, so a successor re-lands from a preserved record — the WI-540 -> WI-551 precedent, proven on the 2026-08-31 run.\n3037-\n3038-THE MECHANICS (c) MUST CLEAR, measured this session:\n3039-- the verdict gate stands down for a partial close BY DESIGN: `_verdict_gate` is keyed off the outcome, and only `merged` owes an APPROVE (\"an approval of nothing\" otherwise); a handback owes the report instead (`_partial_report_refusal`). So (c) does not fight the gate that stopped the lane.\n3040-- BUT the lane closed itself COMPLETE on its own branch (spec at docs/archive/work/complete/, active/ empty there), and the integrator reads the outcome off the spec's directory — as it stands the branch asserts `merged` and the gate applies. The (c) execution therefore first CONVERTS the close: revert the lane's own close commit (or re-perform the close into partial/) so the outcome reads handback and the report mints the disposition row through the ordinary path. No bespoke rename, per OI-70's direction.\n3041-- the merge that lands the partial work meets the six generated-file conflicts of the 2026-08-31 handoff §1.4; the precedented remedy (take trunk's generated side, splice log.md as base + lane entries + trunk entries) is on the branch itself at 52faa5d8.\n3042-\n3043-CARRIED FROM OPTION (b) REGARDLESS OF (c): the two `trace.py --approve modified` renderer defects are filed as their own trunk rows — they caused two of round 019's three MAJORs and reproduce on any lane that regenerates the brief, successor included. The SR-163 shape is ruled by OI-72, not argued in another round.\n3044-\n3045-The lane ran 2026-08-30 02:41-08:59: 43 commits, sessions 001-009+, review sessions numbered to 019 with thirteen round files, an APPROVE at round 010 and eight further rounds that re-opened it. Round 019 returned three MAJORs and the supervising session applied its stop rule rather than redraw:\n3046-- two are defects of `trace.py --approve modified` ON TRUNK, not in the lane - a `Drafted` row rendered as `approved - re-attestation owed`, and a changed `Method` cell truncated in the brief. They are unfiled work, and they will reproduce on any lane that regenerates the brief.\n3047-- one is the SR-163 tension (OI-72): round 013 removed a TC from SR-163 and round 019 asked for it back. The same reviewer has now asked for both sides.\n3048-\n3049-State today: 43 ahead of trunk, 243 behind. The compiled `docs/reviews/WI-508-REVIEW-A.md` and every round file live on the branch and NOT on trunk; the branch was pushed to origin on 2026-08-31 (`origin/wi508-architectural-remap-HELD-for-owner-verdict`), so the record is no longer single-copy. The claim directory `docs/work/active/wi508-architectural-remap/` is still on trunk, which is why the scheduler reports the row `ready` at the head of the frontier while the dispatcher silently skips it (no queued spec, no matching ref) - the two disagree, and the frontier's head is a row the loop can never take.\"\"\"\n3050-blast_radius = \"\"\"The row is `safety_class = spine` and answers SR-163; the deferred WI-543 answers the same SR, and the trajectory check already warns that both are open on it. Every week the lane waits, its nineteen rounds of review evidence age against a trunk that has moved a long way (the armed complexity sensor, the SLOC ratchet re-base, the reverted retention layer), so a resume means a refresh onto a materially different tree and a fresh question about what the old rounds still cover. Abandoning it discards the evidence with the branch unless the record is landed first.\"\"\"\n3051-options = \"\"\"- (a) TAKE THE OWNER'S OWN VERDICT: rename the ref back, accept the lane on the record, merge through `integrate.py integrate`. FOR: the work is reviewed nineteen times over and two of the three blocking findings are not about this lane. AGAINST: merges a lane whose last verdict is CHANGES-REQUESTED, which is the rung standing down by fiat rather than by evidence.\n3052-- (b) FIX THE TWO TRUNK-SIDE RENDERER DEFECTS FIRST, refresh the lane onto current trunk, then draw ONE round on a route the owner will accept, and merge if it approves. FOR: removes two of the three MAJORs at their root, and the remaining one is ruled by OI-72 rather than argued round by round. AGAINST: one more refresh and one more round of wall-clock, on a lane 243 commits behind.\n3053-- (c) RE-SCOPE: close the lane `partial` through the kit's own path, let it mint a disposition row, and have the successor re-land the reviewed parts from a preserved patch (the WI-540 precedent, now proven). FOR: uses the machinery instead of a bespoke hold; the queue moves. AGAINST: spends the review evidence and pays a rebuild of work that was already approved once.\n3054-- (d) ABANDON the lane and re-derive later. FOR: nothing to reconcile. AGAINST: discards a spine program's only execution.\"\"\"\n3055-recommendation = \"\"\"Superseded by the owner direction: (c). Next steps in order: (1) file the two renderer-defect rows (trunk-side, independent of the lane); (2) convert the lane's complete-close to a partial close as above and let the handback report, merge and disposition row run through the kit's own path; (3) the disposition's adjudicator judges keep/discard under OI-70's bounds and queues the successor — which inherits OI-72's ruling for the SR-163 shape and re-lands the reviewed spine content (about 4 lines of low-level-requirements.toml and 10 of test-cases.toml, both auto-merging clean per the handoff §1.4). CLARITY ANSWERED (owner, 2026-08-31): this close is a special case for how the lane got lost — perform the partial close MANUALLY (the complete-close conversion above), then open a successor WI pointing at the preserved record, provided the churn does not risk degrading the output. That risk was assessed this session: the successor must NOT re-land the docs/archive/last_approved bytes from the branch; it re-lands the LIVE registry edits and regenerates the snapshot with the kit's only sanctioned writer (intake.py snapshot) at its own approval commit. Decision 9 measured that writer reproducing the lane's snapshot byte-identical from live state, so regenerate-not-copy carries no degradation risk and the handoff §1.6 snapshot question stays a historical note on the held branch rather than a live hazard on trunk.\"\"\"\n3056-wi_refs = [\"WI-508\", \"WI-543\"]\n3057-\n3058-[open_item.OI-72]\n3059-title = \"SR-163: how an Approved, Test-verified requirement with no complete TC is satisfied\"\n3060-status = \"ruled\"\n3061-ruled_date = \"2026-08-31\"\n3062-ruling_ref = \"docs/log.d/2026-08-31-owner-rulings-oi70-71-72.md\"\n3063-raised = \"2026-08-31\"\n3064-one_line = \"\"\"RULED (the tolerant requirement-reference cell, the four-class checker warn-first, the direct TC proving the checker; WI-543 re-scoped owns SR-163) 2026-08-31 — the owner: the debate over-analyzed it; the expectation is at least one TC tying a template file's production back to a system requirement — even a presence check suffices. Original brief: rule whether SR-163 keeps a directly-tracing TC, is covered only through its LLR arms, or has its Verification class amended - two review rounds of the same reviewer asked for opposite answers\"\"\"\n3065-decision = \"\"\"RULED 2026-08-31 (record docs/log.d/2026-08-31-owner-rulings-oi70-71-72.md): the owner — SR-163 was being over-analyzed; the expectation is at least one test case tying back into the production of a file that is in the template, even a presence check tying the file's reason to a system requirement. The confirmed shape (the owner: \"then you would add those SRs, and then a test case would just do a cheap test to verify the SR exists and the file exists effectively, and that is the TC that would tie up to SR-163\" — confirmed with the checker/TC split, then \"Yes that all sounds good, consider all ruled\"): references mostly cite EXISTING SRs, a new SR minted only where a shipped file has no justifying requirement; the CHECKER does the cheap verify (reference resolves, file exists, bare entries named) over the real inventory on every run; the direct TC proves the checker catches each of the four classes on a scaffold, so the checker's green carries the standing every-file-maps claim and the TC claims exactly what it exercises. The shipped default for unmapped stays warn-only.\n3066-\n3067-MEASURED AGAINST THE REQUIREMENT'S OWN TEXT (same session), the direction is buildable without either horn of the dilemma below. SR-163 names FOUR finding classes reported under the DECLARED WARNING-TO-GATING POLICY: missing files, stale inventory entries, unresolved requirement references, unmapped files. Two are delivered (the dogfood walk and the bootstrap checks — TC-199/TC-200's evidence). The other two CANNOT OCCUR YET because the MAPPING inventory carries no requirement-reference cell — exactly the undischarged arm LLR-203 records. Because the warn-to-gate dial is in the SR's own text, the honest minimal build is: (1) add the requirement-reference carrier to the inventory and a checker for all four classes; (2) declare unmapped and unresolved WARN initially; (3) mint the direct TC that drives each class on a scaffold — a file with no mapping is REPORTED, which is the true state, so the TC claims exactly what it exercises. Round 013's false-coverage objection and round 019's direct-TC ask BOTH dissolve, and no derived-TC type is needed for this SR. The authoring pass — filling a reference for every inventory row — becomes a visible warn-count burn-down instead of a precondition, which re-scopes the deferred WI-543 from \"author the whole join first\" to mechanism + burn-down. The wi508 branch's four Drafted rows stay honest as-is (they verify the delivered arms only).\n3068-\n3069-A GENERAL RULE THE OWNER RECALLED, found NOWHERE in the repo (searched the rulings, the log and the process masters this session): an LLR can be satisfied by its parent, but an SR cannot be satisfied by its children unless the children span the SR's full dimensional space and are not interdependent. Not load-bearing for SR-163 under the direction above (its children do not span its space today, and the direct TC makes the question moot here), but worth writing into the spine-authoring doctrine as its own act if wanted.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a148e95e-cb6b-4656-8f3a-e7379c81b6de","uuid":"293b4d00-5c37-4d64-983b-bee4117b9e8c","timestamp":"2026-09-01T20:43:51.840Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"WI-555-wi508-partial-close.md\n---OI-71---\n3029:[open_item.OI-71]\n3030-title = \"The held wi508 lane: merge on the record, one more round, re-scope, or aba